NAME
       sh - the UC.OS command shell

SYNOPSIS
       sh [-abCefimnuvx] [-o option] [-c command [name [arg...]]
          | -s [arg...] | file [arg...]]

DESCRIPTION
       sh is a POSIX shell written for UC.OS. External programs are
       started with spawn(); builtins, functions and compound
       commands run inside the shell itself, with redirections
       applied to the shell's own file descriptors and undone
       afterwards. Subshells, ( list ) and $(list), also run inside
       the shell: what they change is put back when they end.

       With -c, sh runs command. With a file, it runs the script.
       Otherwise it reads standard input. If standard input is a
       terminal the shell is interactive: it reads /etc/profile and
       then ~/.shrc, and edits lines with the built-in line editor.
       When standard input is not a terminal, commands are read line
       by line with no prompt.

OPTIONS
       Every letter of set can be given to sh, with - to turn it on
       and + to turn it off; -o name likewise.
       -c command   run command; the next arguments become $0, $1...
       -s           read commands from standard input; the operands
                    become $1...
       -i           interactive even if stdin is not a terminal

SET OPTIONS
       -a allexport   export every variable that is assigned
       -C noclobber   > does not overwrite a file (use >|)
       -e errexit     exit when a command fails, except in an if,
                      while or until condition, before && or ||,
                      or after !
       -f noglob      no file name expansion
       -m monitor     job control (on in an interactive shell when
                      the kernel can stop programs)
       -n noexec      read commands, do not run them
       -u nounset     an unset variable is an error
       -v verbose     print input as it is read
       -x xtrace      print each command before running it
          pipefail    a pipeline fails if any stage fails
       set -o lists them; set +o prints commands that restore them.

WORDS AND QUOTING
       'single'     everything literal
       "double"     $ ` and \ keep their meaning, nothing else
       \c           the character c, literally
       # comment    to the end of the line

EXPANSIONS
       In order: tilde, parameters, command substitution, arithmetic,
       field splitting (on $IFS, default space tab newline), pathname
       expansion, quote removal.

       ~ ~/dir ~user        home directories
       $name ${name}        a variable
       $? $$ $! $# $0..$9   status, shell pid, last background pid,
                            number of arguments, arguments
       $- $LINENO $PPID     options in effect, current line, the
                            parent's pid
       $OPTIND $OPTARG      for getopts
       "$@" "$*"            all arguments, separately or joined
       ${#name}             length
       ${name:-word}        word if name is unset or empty
       ${name:=word}        same, and assign it
       ${name:+word}        word if name is set and not empty
       ${name:?word}        error if unset or empty
       ${name#pat} ${name##pat}   remove shortest / longest prefix
       ${name%pat} ${name%%pat}   remove shortest / longest suffix
       $(command) `command`       output of command
       $((expression))            integer arithmetic, C operators
       * ? [abc] [a-z] [!x]       file names (dotfiles need a dot)

COMMANDS
       a | b | c            pipeline; the status is the last one's
       ! pipeline           negate the status
       a ; b                one after the other
       a && b   a || b      b only if a succeeded / failed
       a &                  in the background: prints the pid
       { list; }            group, in this shell
       ( list )             subshell: changes to variables, the
                            directory and descriptors are undone
       if list; then list; [elif list; then list;] [else list;] fi
       while list; do list; done
       until list; do list; done
       for name [in word...]; do list; done
       case word in pattern [| pattern]) list ;; ... esac
       name() { list; }     function; arguments are $1...

REDIRECTIONS
       <file  >file  >>file  <>file   input, output, append, both
       2>file  2>&1  n>&m  n>&-       other descriptors
       &>file  &>>file                stdout and stderr together
       <<WORD  <<-WORD  <<'WORD'      here-documents

BUILTINS
       cd [dir|-]  pwd  echo [-neE]  printf format [args]
       exit [n]  return [n]  break [n]  continue [n]  shift [n]
       export [name[=value]]  unset [-f] name  local name[=value]
       readonly [-p] name[=value]...
       set [options] [-- args]   (no arguments: list variables)
       alias [name=value]  unalias name|-a
       source file [args]  . file [args]  eval args
                            (. and source look for a name without /
                            in PATH, then in the current directory)
       command [-p] [-v|-V] name [args]   run name, skipping
                            functions; -v / -V say what name is
       getopts optstring name [args]
       read [-r] [-p prompt] [name...]
       test expr  [ expr ]   (-e -f -d -s -x -z -n = != -eq -lt...)
       true  false  :  type name  history [n|-c]
       times                this shell's CPU time
       trap [action] condition...   EXIT (0) and signal names or
                            numbers; '' ignores, - resets; no
                            operands lists the traps
       kill [-s sig | -sig] pid|job...   kill -l [status]
       wait [pid|job...]  jobs [-l|-p]  fg [job]  bg [job]
       disown [job]  umask [-S] [mode]  help
       exec >file           make redirections permanent
       exec command         replace the shell with command; on a
                            kernel without exec, and inside a
                            subshell, command runs as a child and
                            the shell exits with its status
       A job is %n, %%, %+, %-, %string or %?string. Special builtins
       (. : break continue eval exec exit export readonly return set
       shift times trap unset) are found before functions; an error
       in one ends a script with status 2.

LINE EDITOR
       On a terminal: Left/Right, Home/End, Backspace/Delete,
       Ctrl-A/E start/end, Ctrl-B/F left/right, Ctrl-K/U kill to
       end/start, Ctrl-W kill a word, Ctrl-Y yank, Alt-B/F word
       left/right, Up/Down or Ctrl-P/N history, Tab completes
       commands (from $PATH) and file names, Ctrl-L clears the
       screen, Ctrl-C drops the line, Ctrl-D on an empty line exits.
       History is kept in ~/.sh_history.

PROMPT
       PS1 is the prompt, PS2 the continuation prompt. Escapes:
       \u user, \h host, \w directory (~ for home), \W its last
       part, \$ $ or #, \n newline, \e escape, \[ \] around colour
       codes. Example, an orange chevron:

              PS1='\[\e[1;38;2;254;128;25m\]› \[\e[0m\]'

JOB CONTROL
       An interactive shell starts a new session. Each foreground
       pipeline gets its own process group and the terminal's
       foreground group, so Ctrl-C ends the pipeline and not the
       shell. When the shell itself is busy (a loop of builtins, a
       function), Ctrl-C stops the whole command line: $? becomes
       130 and the prompt returns; a script exits with status 130.

       When the kernel can stop programs, Ctrl-Z stops the
       foreground job and prints [n]+ Stopped; fg brings a job back
       and bg lets it run in the background; a background job that
       ends or stops is reported before the next prompt. On a kernel
       without that support, set -m is refused with a message,
       Ctrl-Z does nothing, kill(1) ends a process and the shell
       reports it as killed.

SIGNALS
       Traps on signals run between commands. On a kernel that
       delivers no signals only the EXIT trap runs, and a trap for a
       signal the shell sends to itself with kill.

FILES
       /etc/profile   read first by an interactive shell
       ~/.shrc        then this; your dotfile
       ~/.sh_history  command history

EXIT STATUS
       The status of the last command; 127 for a command not found,
       126 if it could not be started, 128+n if killed by signal n,
       2 for a syntax error.

BUGS
       No fork means some things differ from other shells: a
       background & list of builtins or compound commands runs in
       the foreground; the last stage of a pipeline runs in this
       shell, so `echo x | read v` sets v; two builtin stages of a
       pipeline run one after the other, so an endless first stage
       never ends.

SEE ALSO
       bc(1), unix-club(7)

